==============================================================================
PART 0.  gamma <-> delta, checked against exact big integers
==============================================================================
  L = log2 3 = 1.584962500721156181453739...
  gamma := K - log2 q,  q = 2^K - 3^n > 0,  K = ceil(n L),
  delta := K - n L in (0,1)   =>   gamma = -log2(1 - 2^-delta).
  gamma = -log2(1-2^-delta) verified against K - log2(2^K - 3^n) at 12 exponents; worst deviation 1.282e-36
  K = ceil(n L) is realized exactly as (3**n).bit_length(): verified for n = 1..399

==============================================================================
PART 1.  THE GAMMA BUDGET -- what growth of gamma(p) the claim needs
==============================================================================
  Trim(c0,A):  gamma + log2 p > c0 * n / p^A,   c0 = 0.1157 (period 3).
  Refuted at p by a size-passer with gamma(p) + log2 p <= c0*n(p)/p^A,
  and the staircase forces n(p) >= 0.68 * 1.585^p.

  gamma(p) growth law       A=0   A=1   A=2   A=3   A=5   A=10     <- smallest p0 with (REFUTE) for all p >= p0
  2.5*log2 p   (observed)      11    18    26    35    56   116
  p                            12    19    28    38    59   120
  p^2                          19    28    37    48    71   133
  2^sqrt(p)                    12    19    29    39    63   128
  1.2^p                        11    21    35    52    90   200
  1.5^p                        47   136   246   368   632  1356
  1.585^p       (control)    none  none  none  none  none  none
  0.05*1.585^p  (control)      11  none  none  none  none  none

  Reading: every law with limsup log2(gamma(p))/p < log2 1.585 = 0.66446
  defeats Trim(c0,A) for EVERY A from an explicit p0; the two controls
  at growth rate 1.585^p defeat only finitely many A (or none).

  The exact threshold. (REFUTE) for all A holds iff
      gamma(p) * p^A / 1.585^p -> 0   for every fixed A,
  i.e. iff log2 gamma(p) <= 0.66446*p - omega(log p).  The weakest
  SIMPLE sufficient bound is therefore
      gamma(p) = O(rho^p)   for any fixed rho < 1.585,
  and a fortiori gamma(p) = 2^o(p), O(p^B), O(p), O(log p).
  O(log p) is NOT needed for this job; O(p) has room to spare.

  What the stronger readings need (the ladder):
    J1  refute every polynomial-in-p trim   (the published sentence)
        -> gamma(p) <= rho^p, any rho < 1.585.      [subexponential]
    J2  refute every subexponential trim c0*n/2^o(p)
        -> gamma(p) = 2^o(p).                        [subexponential]
    J3  Theorem 12.8.1's rate 1.585^-p sharp up to a polynomial factor
        -> gamma(p) = O(p^B).                        [polynomial]
    J4  ... sharp up to a factor O(log p)
        -> gamma(p) = O(log p).                      [logarithmic]
  Only J4 needs the published hedge's own O(log p).

  Quantified J3/J4: with n(p) = kappa*1.585^p, a size-passer of gamma(p)
  caps every valid uniform trim constant c(p) (in gamma' > c(p)*n) at
      c(p) <= (gamma(p) + log2 p) / (kappa * 1.585^p).
  gamma(p)=O(log p) gives c(p) = O(log p * 1.585^-p) -- Theorem 12.8.1's
  own 0.585/(1.585^p - 1) is then sharp to within a factor O(log p).
  gamma(p)=O(p) gives O(p * 1.585^-p): sharp to within a factor O(p).

==============================================================================
PART 2a.  The continued fraction of L, its signs, and the desert
==============================================================================
  514 convergents computed (q up to ~10^265);
  sign h_i - q_i L > 0 exactly at odd i; sandwich 1/(q_i+q_{i+1}) < |h_i - q_i L| < 1/q_{i+1} holds throughout.
  partial quotients a_0..a_29: [1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 5, 2, 23, 2, 2, 1, 1, 55, 1, 4, 3, 1, 1, 15, 1, 9, 2, 5, 7, 1, 1, 4, 8]

  Correctly-signed (s>0) convergent denominators up to 10^9:
    [1, 5, 41, 306, 15601, 79335, 190537, 10781274, 171928773, 397573379]
  The flagged gap q_13 = 190537 -> q_14 = 10590737 (ratio 55.6, a_14 = 55).
  Rhin (12.5.3): ||nL|| > 0.003154 * n^-13.3 -- holds at all 514 convergents, worst
  slack factor 1.316e+02 (the convergents are where the bound is tightest).

  Convergent-desert scan over the recipe's own window [0.68*1.585^p, 3.5*1.585^p]:
    periods with NO correctly-signed convergent denominator in window: [5, 9, 14, 15, 16, 17, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 36, 37, 38, 44]
    (this is the availability gap of 12.8.6.1, re-derived here; note
     the correctly-signed SEMIconvergent runs do not repair it: the run
     following q_13 has length a_15 = 1, i.e. one member.)

==============================================================================
PART 2b.  Exact quality laws: multiples and semiconvergents
==============================================================================
  (i) MULTIPLES.  For a correctly-signed convergent q_k (s_k = h_k - q_k L
      > 0) and t with t*theta_k < 1/2:   K - nL = t*theta_k EXACTLY at
      n = t*q_k, K = t*h_k.  Checked below against ceil(nL) directly.
      verified on 3376 (k,t) instances, 0 failures.

  (ii) SEMICONVERGENTS.  n_j = q_(k-1) + j q_k, K_j = h_(k-1) + j h_k,
      j = 1..a_(k+1), at a correctly-signed q_(k-1):
          K_j - n_j L  =  theta_(k-1) - j*theta_k   EXACTLY,
      strictly decreasing in j, positive throughout the run, and equal
      to theta_(k+1) at j = a_(k+1) (the next correctly-signed
      convergent). Worst case over j is j = a_(k+1): quality theta_(k+1),
      i.e. the LARGEST gamma of the run; the smallest gamma of the run is
      at j = 1, quality theta_(k-1) - theta_k.
      verified on 44 (k,j) instances, 0 failures.
      correctly-signed run lengths a_(k+1) at k-1 = q_1=1:2, q_3=5:3, q_5=41:5, q_7=306:23, q_9=15601:2, q_11=79335:1, q_13=190537:1, q_15=10781274:3, q_17=171928773:1, q_19=397573379:1, q_21=6586818670:2
      -> runs are SHORT wherever the next partial quotient is small, so
         semiconvergents do NOT blanket the desert additively; the
         additive blanket is supplied by multiples and, unconditionally,
         by the covering bound of Part 2c.

==============================================================================
PART 2c.  The window-covering bound (three distances), verified
==============================================================================
  CLAIM. Let k = max{i : q_i <= N/2}. For every N0, the N points
  {nL}, n = N0..N0+N-1, have all circle gaps <= theta_(k-1) < 1/q_k.
  Hence every arc of length > theta_(k-1) contains some {nL}.
  2798 windows tested (N = 2..699 at four offsets, plus seven large N);
  violations: 0; worst observed ratio maxgap/theta_(k-1) = 1.000000 (equality is attained, so the bound is sharp).

  NEGATIVE CONTROL. The bound must FAIL to certify coverage when the
  target arc is shorter than theta_(k-1). Checked by exhibiting, for
  each of several N, an arc of length exactly theta_(k-1) containing no
  point of the window (the maximal gap itself):
  4/4 maximal gaps confirmed empty -- the covering threshold is not
  an artifact of a loose estimate; below it, coverage genuinely fails.

==============================================================================
PART 2d.  Certified gamma ceiling at every period (availability)
==============================================================================
  For each p: N = #integers in the window; k = max{i: q_i <= N/2};
  eps_min = 2*theta_(k-1); gamma_ceiling = log2(1/eps_min) + 0.5288.
  Every gamma in [1.77, gamma_ceiling] is then prescribable to within
  1.28 bits by choosing eps in [2*theta_(k-1), 1/2].  UNCONDITIONAL:
  the only input is the (computed, finite) continued fraction of L.

